  titleHome: 'YYDB — single-file local database',

  homeEyebrow: 'YYDB · EMBEDDED LOCAL DATABASE',
  homeHeroLine1: 'One file,',
  homeHeroLine2: 'one database.',
  homeLede: 'YYDB embeds in your application process and keeps the schema and the data in one .yydb file. Queries are written in VOS, and there is no database service to deploy.',
  homeActionInstall: 'Install YYDB',
  homeActionDocs: 'Read the docs',
  homeInstallHint: 'Install once, then open a local file.',
  homeEdition: 'LOCAL-FIRST / OPEN SOURCE',

  homePrinciple1Title: 'In-process',
  homePrinciple1Body: 'The database links into your application as a library. Reads and writes are plain function calls, with no server process.',
  homePrinciple2Title: 'One file',
  homePrinciple2Body: 'Schema and data live in the same .yydb file. A backup is a file copy.',
  homePrinciple3Title: 'VOS queries',
  homePrinciple3Body: 'Schema definitions and read-write operations share one VOS language. There is no second query dialect to learn.',

  homeAnatomySchemaTitle: 'Schema travels with the file',
  homeAnatomySchemaBody: 'Tables are written in VOS and stored inside the file, so the CLI, clients and the engine all read the same definition.',
  homeAnatomyWalTitle: 'Committed writes survive',
  homeAnatomyWalBody: 'Writes are appended to the WAL with a commit sequence and folded into data pages at checkpoint. Reopen the file after a crash and the unfolded part replays automatically.',

  homeWriteTitle: 'Every write travels\nthe same route.',
  homeWriteBody: 'Every commit is appended to the WAL with its commit sequence and folded into data pages at checkpoint. The path is fixed, and no write ever takes a second route.',

  homeHostsTitle: 'Four hosts,\none file.',
  homeHostsBody: 'Node.js, Rust, the CLI and browser WASM sessions all read and write the same .yydb file. The semantics come from one Rust core, so no format conversion is involved.',

  homeCodeLink: 'Read the query guide',

  homeClosingTitle: 'Start with one file.',
  homeClosingBody: 'Install @yydb/yydb, run your first VOS statement and keep the data in your own file.',
  homeClosingAction: 'Read the quickstart',
